WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.452
Jev needs Jeeves.

00:00:01.452 --> 00:00:03.147
So does your to-do list.

00:00:03.147 --> 00:00:06.942
Here is a little direction, for work with a plot twist.

00:00:07.642 --> 00:00:08.869
A request comes in.

00:00:08.869 --> 00:00:13.196
Jeeves gives each step a job, and makes the whole process visible.

00:00:13.196 --> 00:00:16.618
This is a real workflow, running with Jev and Codex.

00:00:17.318 --> 00:00:20.912
Jev sees a concrete outage and chooses the urgent route.

00:00:20.912 --> 00:00:25.276
You can inspect the answer, confidence, and the branch it selected.

00:00:25.976 --> 00:00:29.348
But when a request is vague, guessing is poor form.

00:00:29.348 --> 00:00:34.242
Jeeves asks for the missing facts, saves the progress, and waits for you.

00:00:34.942 --> 00:00:36.631
Add the context and submit.

00:00:36.631 --> 00:00:39.634
Only then does the next agent pick up the work.

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:46.467
Your clarification becomes a concrete action brief, with a suggested fix and a draft reply.

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:48.084
The facts stay visible.

00:00:48.084 --> 00:00:49.634
Nothing has been sent.

00:00:50.334 --> 00:00:56.020
Keep the workflow for next time, or export it as a portable skill with its requirements.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.798
A useful process deserves a second outing.

00:00:59.498 --> 00:01:00.925
Jev makes the judgment.

00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:03.096
Jeeves handles the follow-through.

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:04.213
Meet your Jeeves.

